1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,440
A fantasy projection can look like one
exact answer.

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:07,650
But the better question is: how much
downside and upside sits

3
00:00:07,660 --> 00:00:11,430
around that number? In this walkthrough,
I’ll show you how to

4
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:15,400
use Propeller’s MLB Fantasy board on the
web and mobile app,

5
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:18,200
how to read floor, projection, and
ceiling,

6
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:21,830
and how to compare two hitters with
Sit/Start.

7
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:24,640
Start by opening Propeller and choosing
Fantasy.

8
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:28,230
The current public release is built for
MLB hitters,

9
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:30,900
using DraftKings MLB scoring.

10
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:35,420
Before comparing anyone, confirm the
sport, slate date,

11
00:00:35,620 --> 00:00:38,020
scoring label, and update status.

12
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:42,850
Those details matter because the available
market inputs can change during

13
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:46,860
the day. The board gives every listed
hitter three numbers:

14
00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:49,640
floor, projected points, and ceiling.

15
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:53,040
Projected points is the central estimate.

16
00:00:53,740 --> 00:00:56,800
Propeller builds it from available current
player markets,

17
00:00:57,240 --> 00:01:01,630
then uses recent performance for scoring
components that those markets do

18
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:05,650
not cover. The goal is to turn several
pieces of information

19
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:10,670
into one estimated fantasy score under the
scoring system shown on

20
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:15,370
the page. Each player card also shows how
many market inputs

21
00:01:15,380 --> 00:01:17,510
and recent games support the estimate.

22
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:22,030
Those counts give you useful context, but
they are not quality

23
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:26,070
grades. More inputs can mean more of the
projection is connected

24
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:30,090
to today’s available markets; it does not
mean the outcome is

25
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:34,550
certain. Floor is the lower end of the
player’s expected scoring

26
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:37,460
range. Ceiling is the upper end.

27
00:01:37,980 --> 00:01:41,010
These ranges use the player’s recent
scoring variation,

28
00:01:41,020 --> 00:01:43,510
centered around today’s projection.

29
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:47,320
They are not guaranteed minimums or
maximums.

30
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:50,730
A real result can finish below the floor
or above the

31
00:01:50,740 --> 00:01:54,140
ceiling. You can sort the board three
ways.

32
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:57,160
Choose Projected for the central ranking
of the slate.

33
00:01:57,920 --> 00:02:00,350
Choose Floor when you want to see which
hitters carry the

34
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:05,550
strongest lower-range estimates. Choose
Ceiling to find the largest upper-range

35
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:09,420
outcomes. No single sort is automatically
best.

36
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:11,860
Each one answers a different question.

37
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:16,850
For example, Projected is a practical
starting point for an overall

38
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:21,140
comparison. Floor brings downside
protection into focus.

39
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:24,100
Ceiling is useful when you are looking for
upside.

40
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:28,470
The important part is knowing which lens
you selected before you

41
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:30,280
interpret the order of the list.

42
00:02:31,180 --> 00:02:32,880
Now let’s compare two hitters.

43
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:36,160
Select the first player, then select a
second.

44
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:40,930
Propeller opens the Sit/Start comparison
and places the player with the

45
00:02:40,940 --> 00:02:43,400
higher projected score in the Start
position.

46
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:47,750
The other player appears as Sit, and the
point difference shows

47
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:50,700
how far apart their central projections
are.

48
00:02:51,540 --> 00:02:54,300
The comparison also keeps both ranges
visible.

49
00:02:54,940 --> 00:02:56,440
That context is important.

50
00:02:57,180 --> 00:03:01,090
Two players can have nearly identical
projections but very different floors

51
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:04,110
and ceilings. One may have a tighter
range,

52
00:03:04,120 --> 00:03:07,520
while the other offers more upside with
more downside.

53
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:11,010
A large ceiling does not erase a low
floor,

54
00:03:11,020 --> 00:03:14,950
and a higher floor does not guarantee the
better final score.

55
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:20,910
Treat Sit/Start as a direct comparison
under the displayed scoring system—not

56
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:22,980
as a complete lineup optimizer.

57
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:25,360
It does not know every league setting,

58
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:30,500
contest rule, roster constraint, injury
update, weather condition,

59
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:32,260
or late lineup change.

60
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:36,190
It is designed to make the projection
difference and the uncertainty

61
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:38,300
around it easier to understand.

62
00:03:39,540 --> 00:03:42,180
The same workflow is available in the
mobile app.

63
00:03:42,580 --> 00:03:46,850
Open Fantasy, confirm the MLB slate, then
use the Floor,

64
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:49,880
Projected, or Ceiling chips to change the
ranking.

65
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:53,410
Tap two hitters to open the mobile
Sit/Start card.

66
00:03:53,420 --> 00:03:57,240
You will see the recommendation,
projected-point gap,

67
00:03:57,540 --> 00:04:01,950
each player’s range, and the amount of
market and recent-game information

68
00:04:01,960 --> 00:04:06,360
supporting the comparison. Because today’s
inputs can move,

69
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:09,040
refresh before making a final decision.

70
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:13,450
The web board updates periodically and
also has a manual refresh

71
00:04:13,460 --> 00:04:16,710
control. On mobile, pull down to refresh.

72
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:20,800
If the board says “latest available”
instead of live,

73
00:04:21,220 --> 00:04:24,820
check the slate date and update time
before relying on it.

74
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:28,190
Here is the full workflow: confirm MLB,

75
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:30,400
the date, and the scoring system.

76
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:33,780
Choose the sort that matches the question
you are asking.

77
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:37,460
Select two hitters. Compare the projected
score,

78
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:39,060
then inspect both ranges.

79
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:44,330
Finally, verify lineup status, weather,
and the rules of your fantasy

80
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:49,810
contest. Propeller’s public Fantasy page
is useful for discovering

81
00:04:49,820 --> 00:04:54,450
the current board, while the full
comparison workflow is available inside

82
00:04:54,460 --> 00:04:56,540
Propeller on web and mobile.

83
00:04:57,200 --> 00:05:00,650
Pitchers and additional sports are not
part of this public release

84
00:05:00,660 --> 00:05:04,510
yet; they will only be added when their
market coverage and

85
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:06,120
projection ranges are ready.

86
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:12,170
Open Propeller Picks to explore today’s
MLB Fantasy projections and try

87
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:14,540
a Sit/Start comparison of your own.

88
00:05:15,140 --> 00:05:18,940
Propeller is an independent research and
analytics platform,

89
00:05:19,380 --> 00:05:23,880
not a sportsbook, and every projection is
an estimate—not a guarantee.

